## Deviations: debt, breakage and application bugs

Every deviation from the rules above is written down here. An undocumented deviation causes one of
two failures: the assistant treats it as the house style and multiplies it, or decides it is a
defect it just introduced and starts repairing working code instead of doing the task.

Four kinds belong here: **broken commands** (with the actual error, and a note that it predates
the current work), **legacy code violating a rule** (by path, labelled *debt, not examples*),
**application bugs** (the test stays, marked expected-to-fail with the reason — never deleted,
never weakened), and **exemplar exceptions** (which part of a named exemplar to copy and which to
ignore).

An expected-failure marker is only for a confirmed product defect. A merely unstable test is never
marked this way — flakiness is investigated. Entries are removed when fixed, not left to rot.

